0.006392652, 0.02245658, 0.021243872, 0.036753654, 0.011291796, 0.021932494, -0.06735408, -0.0058521153, -0.054595117, 0.005815081, -0.035147585, 0.054467488, 0.10443377, -0.01230525, -0.0041391067, -0.05734157, -0.052419852, -0.013319616, 0.021806585, 0.046977036, 0.030256793, -0.018910157, -0.016208509, 0.036200646, -0.16607626, -0.020425715, 0.011220653, 0.06963659, 0.012736774, 0.048654635, 0.009196438, 0.0033798243, -0.026304083, 0.00201548, 0.0009690823, -0.04136793, -0.042856194, 0.050609846, -0.044727508, 0.028254349, -0.017837984, -0.027275158, -0.03046908, -0.12548625, -0.03763512, -0.008642985, 0.10288395, 0.03966687, 0.10236379, 0.029707316, -0.014656931, -0.0666911, -0.036657482, 0.0503075, 0.05960084, 0.0034995568, 0.10161077, 0.004355537, -0.0041637057, 0.02077594, -0.0026016904, -0.0003924296, 0.054146796, -0.024581576, 0.093493305, 0.024881864, 0.09759671, 0.04450101, 0.108486645, -0.034302443, -0.00394658, 0.011062053, -0.0025704987, 0.00971437, 0.020523725, 0.043885622, -0.000019429552, 0.025786398, 0.0012549108, 0.03474956, -0.023715429, 0.025934666, -0.010384322, 0.04443399, 0.009235546, 0.020857194, -0.017180169, -0.0015467297, -0.07694463], embedding_model: 'snowflake-arctic-embed-xs@d8c86521', organism: organism:`whitefly`, source: source:`edis_in286_sweetpotato_whitefly`, text: 'Infestations of whiteflies, especially in greenhouses, may occasionally be a mixture of Bemisia tabaci and Trialeurodes vaporariorum (Westwood), the greenhouse whitefly. They can be differentiated at the adult stage based on the position in which the wings are held over the body while alive, close to the body and tent-like in Bemisia, and more loosely in T. vaporariorum. Another way to differentiate adult Bemisia and T. vaporariorum is to examine the compound eyes using a microscope. The upper and lower compound eyes of T. vaporariorum are completely divided whereas they are joined by one ommatidium in Bemisia. Adult Bemisia are soft and whitish-yellow when they first emerge from their nymphal exuviae. Within a few hours, their two pairs of wings become iridescent white due to the deposition of a powdery wax. The body remains light yellow with a light dusting of wax. The body of the female measures 0.96 mm from the tip of the vertex (head) to the tip of the abdomen, while the male is somewhat smaller at 0.82 mm.', text_hash: '091ce5546d2ce06e198ff90196085d57061a376313304fe7ae6f0525fbe66b74', topic: 'recognition' } RETURN NONE;
