1469, -0.014437075, 0.074267365, 0.03025991, -0.086516455, -0.015348562, 0.015038659, -0.0026097975, -0.06338459, 0.013632458, 0.0050957887, -0.045952916, 0.08641359, 0.0043654814, 0.042586297, 0.04629531, -0.027216911, 0.0037773643, -0.08533532, 0.034393255, -0.055933774, 0.026973067, 0.00814588, -0.07371856, 0.05325845, -0.03144014, 0.0041520894, -0.17638576, 0.0064787096, 0.014624848, -0.010321914, -0.019541372, 0.094496064, 0.0460205, -0.031045524, 0.031981938, 0.007513525, 0.043310486, -0.037267342, -0.0083638765, -0.068080775, -0.046483684, 0.04341191, -0.050948348, -0.021993374, 0.01662472, -0.0991019, 0.039440762, 0.043805093, 0.14270835, -0.012105347, 0.03565985, 0.018741839, 0.0066565354, 0.028923335, -0.04179209, 0.08139287, 0.06123506, -0.028270716, 0.05170016, 0.044939596, -0.031473264, 0.02645759, 0.09656706, -0.03523727, -0.004420942, 0.022018997, 0.07091278, -0.030895324, 0.021572078, -0.015951224, 0.12620609, 0.030100977, 0.003523222, -0.016116964, -0.049104065, -0.027618287, -0.0004639843, -0.032754924, -0.024209881, -0.0007837586, 0.0041731517, 0.0747162, 0.033203885, -0.000038112965, 0.0013152653, 0.0065158745, -0.052860748, -0.024145482, -0.02111144, -0.04753902, 0.005229636], embedding_model: 'snowflake-arctic-embed-xs@d8c86521', source: source:`ontario_tomato_bacterial_diseases`, text: 'Fixed copper bactericides are currently the only effective registered control products for bacterial disease on tomatoes. However, bacterial speck populations in Ontario have shown widespread resistance to copper, and in some areas of the US, bacterial spot has also developed resistance. Despite this, copper is still a useful tool for managing bacterial spot and canker in Ontario.\nResearch at Ridgetown College (University of Guelph), Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada and elsewhere has shown that foliar-applied fixed copper sprays will reduce the number of bacterial cells on tomato foliage. Depending on the amount of bacteria present and the size and rate of growth of the plants, spray intervals of 7 days or less may be required. Copper sprays are less effective, to the point of ineffectiveness, when spray intervals are extended. Good spray coverage and the use of recommended rates are also very important.', text_hash: '49fabbd5abdc4808d05bed76188199b318ee19a87f62b706121227349cd67d23', topic: 'management' } RETURN NONE;
